1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nc4 Nxe4 5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Be7 ({Much better is} 6... g6 {as played by Alexander against Matanovic in the next round.}) 7. d4 O-O 8. Bd3 Nd7 9. O-O f5 10. Ne3 Nb6 11. f4 Be6 12. Re1 Qd7 13. c4 g6 14. d5 Bf7 15. Bb2 Na4 16. Bd4 Rae8 ({There was no time for this. The wretched-looking} 16... Be8 {was necessary...}) 17. Rb1 (17. g4 $5 {Milner-Barry}) 17... Nc5 18. Ba1 $2 ({Stockfish 17:} 18. g4 $1 $16) 18... Qd8 $8 19. Bxf5 Bf6 20. Bh3 $6 (20. Bd3 $11) 20... Bxa1 21. Rxa1 Qf6 22. Ng4 $2 Rxe1+ 23. Qxe1 Qd4+ (23... Qxf4 $2 24. Qc3 $11) 24. Kh1 Kg7 $17 (24... Qxf4 $5 $19) 25. Rd1 Re8 26. Qf1 Qe4 27. Qf2 $5 h5 $6 ({Stockfish 17:} 27... Qxc4 $1 $19) 28. Ne5 $5 dxe5 29. Qxc5 (29. fxe5 $5) 29... exf4 30. Rf1 Qe3 $2 (30... Qe5 $17 {Milner-Barry}) ({Stockfish 17:} 30... b6 $1) 31. Qxc7 Re7 32. Qa5 b6 33. Qb4 $11 Re4 34. Qb2+ Kg8 35. d6 $2 (35. Qf6 $1 $14) 35... Qe2 $2 ({Stockfish 17:} 35... Qd4 $1 $17) 36. Qa1 $1 $16 Qxc4 $2 37. d7 $18 Qd4 38. Rd1 {Source: BCM, November 1951, p308. "A stirring game in which the advantage changed hands with bewildering rapidity; but not so bewildering as the speed with which my opponent conducted both his games in the match. In this game his clock showed 1½ hours for 38 moves; but in the second game the same time for 52 moves, while I was approaching the second time control (3½ hours!)." (Milner-Barry)} 1-0

